>> A4: When two user processes both need a new frame at the same time, >> how
are races avoided?

We have a global frame table struct list frame_table; and a global lock struct
lock frame_table_lock. Our code uses and implements them so that only one
proccess can do operations on the global frame table at a time. We make sure a
process aqcuires the global frame table lock before it does any operations on
the global frame table list. This applies to everything w.r.t frame allocation
including, finding a victim frame to evict, freeing a frame and removing it from
the global list, and adding a new frame to the global list. Without the global
lock 2 processes needing a new frame at the same time might select the same
victim frame, or corrupt the frame table list.

So if two user processes both need a new frame at the same time, the important
section which can cause errors due to race conditions of finding a victim page
or inserting a new frame to the global frame list etc. is done one process at a
time to avoid races.

Additionally, when both processes need a new frame and swapping may happen we
also have a global struct lock swap_lock; so that, operations on the global
struct block *swap_block; and global struct bitmap *swap_table; are also done
one processes at a time, to avoid race conditions that could cause errors
(especially when dealing with global data structures). (Basically similar idea
as for frame table)

