>> A5: Why did you choose the data structure(s) that you did for >> representing
virtual-to-physical mappings?

For the supp_page_table_entry we chose a hash table because of performance.
Every page fault must look up the SPTE entry as quickly as possible to minimize
delays in output. Since a hash table has O(1) lookup time, its far better than a
linked list. We also need to store lookup SPTE based on virtual addresses which
normal arrays can't do so we opted for hash tables. 

we chose to represent / abstract a page worth of physical memory as a frame
object. then an array of these formed a frame table. we chose to do this because
the OS must track which frames are allocated, free, pinned, mapped to which
virtual page, etc, etc. Having an frame table and abstracting each physical page
as an object gives us a space to store all the information we need and makes
processes such as swapping, choosing the evictee, and pinning all possible.

