>> B2: When a frame is required but none is free, some frame must be >> evicted.
Describe your code for choosing a frame to evict.

We implemented the simple variant of the "second chance" algorithm as described
in the memory management lecture notes (Second Chance implementation Version 1 :
FIFO-like algorithm). Within swap.c we have the method get_victim_frame() to
choose the frame to evict. Within the method we iterate through the frame table
which is a linked list by checking the head and moving/swapping the head, then
continuing to the next element which is the new head. If the head's accessed bit
is 0, that means the bit was not accessed recently so we choose this frame to
evict and return it. However before we choose the frame we make sure the frame
is not pinned since we cant evict a pinned frame (e.g it is a frame with user
resources that are used by the kernel). But, if the accessed bit is 1 (recently
accessed) we set the bit to 0 and move it to the tail. Basically if the accessed
bit is 1 we try to find a frame that wasn't recently accessed (accessed bit is
0) to evict instead. Also since the frame is set to 0 and moved to the tail, if
there is no other frame before this one with accessed bit is 0 then this frame
will ultimately end up be evicted. so our algorithm ensures that a victim frame
is chosen and performs at least as well as the simple variant of the "second
chance" since it is the simple variant of second chance algorithm.

>> B3: When a process P obtains a frame that was previously used by a >> process
Q, how do you adjust the page table (and any other data >> structures) to
reflect the frame Q no longer has?

To reflect the frame that Q no longer has we first set the supplemental page
table entry values. Each frame has a struct supp_page_table_entry *spte; to
access the spte mapping to the frame. To reflect that Q no longer has the frame
we first make sure the f->spte->in_memory = false since Q does not have a
physical frame anymore and the page is not in physical memory. We also set
f->spte->frame = NULL. spte->frame is the frame mapping that a spte has, but
since Q no longer has a frame we set it to null. For the page table we also call
pagedir_clear_page (f->owning_thread->pagedir, f->spte->vaddr) to get rid of the
mapping in the page table between virtual memory Q and the physical frame
mapping that Q no longer has. f->owning_thread->pagedir is the page dir related
to the frame and f->spte->vaddr is the user virtual page that we need to get rid
of in the mapping. For a special case as well, when Q no longer has a frame
because it was swapped out/evicted, if f->spte->type == VM_BIN (a binary/
executable type file) and the page is dirty we also need to switch f->spte->type
= VM_ANON for Q. This is because if we want this page again when page
fault/demand paging happens we want to get the dirty/changed page from the swap
parition rather than the old one from the VIM_BIN type file in the disk. Also in
general is the frame was swapped out we set f->spte->swap_slot to the actual
swap slot it was swapped to so we can get back our page when it page faults
again later.

When Q no longer has a frame we also make sure to free the frame with our
frame_free() function. For our global frame table with struct frames, when q no
longer has a frame we make sure to remove the frame from the global frame table
and free the frame struct f related to this frame by doing free(f). We also call
palloc_free_page (f->k_page_addr); f->k_page_addr would be saved before we free
the struct. We call this function to free the actual physical page from Q so
that our vm system knows this phyiscal page is free to be used by a new process
(in this case process P). (Our frame allocation fucntion will also take care of
things when process p obtains the frame)


