>> B5: Explain the basics of your VM synchronization design.  In >> particular,
explain how it prevents deadlock.  (Refer to the >> textbook for an explanation
of the necessary conditions for >> deadlock.)

from text book Four conditions need to hold for a deadlock to occur [C+71]: •
Mutual exclusion: Threads claim exclusive control of resources that they require
(e.g., a thread grabs a lock). • Hold-and-wait: Threads hold resources allocated
to them (e.g., locks that they have already acquired) while waiting for
additional resources (e.g., locks that they wish to acquire). • No preemption:
Resources (e.g., locks) cannot be forcibly removed from threads that are holding
them. • Circular wait: There exists a circular chain of threads such that each
thread holds one or more resources (e.g., locks) that are being requested by the
next thread in the chain.

If any of these four conditions are not met, deadlock cannot occur. Thus, we
first explore techniques to prevent deadlock; each of these strategies seeks to
prevent one of the above conditions from arising and thus is one approach to
handling the deadlock problem.

Our vm synchornization design includes our global frame table with out global
frame table lock, our global swap block and bitmap with our global swap block
and bitmap lock, our file system lock.

All these global locks are created so that when any process wants to touch the
global frame table, swap block, swap bitmap, or file system, they must acquire
the respective locks first. This makes sure that only one process can access the
global resource at a time. Our design prevents deadlocks by preventing
hold-and-wait. Whenever we acquire locks we only try to hold it for the shortest
amount of time necessary and release it so that threads/processes wont be
watiing for another lock while holding a lock. An example of this is in our
frame_function we acquire the frame lock to find our victim frame, then release
it then acquire the filesys lock in one of the cases for file_write_at then
release the file sys lock then reacquire the frame lock to add a new frame. In
this example we never hold and wait and we do this thoughout our project.

Another important part of our synchronization design is pinning. We use pinning
so that any pinned frame cannot be evicted. We use this to stop deadlocks by
stopping hold-and-wait. One example is we pin frames of user resources that the
kernel needs to use so that a page fault wont occur and cause a deadlock. We
mainly implement this for the read and write system calls. For both read and
write syscalls we need to pin all frames inside the buffer. For example if we
call read syscall we will need to acquire the filesys lock and call file_read.
But if the buffer is evicted because it isn't pinned our vm system will page
fault to bring back the buffer pages. But to do that the page fault will try to
acquire the filesys lock again and cause a deadlock. That is why we pin user
resources used such as buffer for read and write syscalls and unpin them oncethe
syscall is done. With pinning the same process won't try to acquire the same
lock its holding so we prevent hold-and-wait.

We also just consider synchronization and deadlocks in general. One example of
this is for the read syscall. Once case where a deadlock can happen is if the
pages of our buffer that we write to for the read syscall is not writable. To
call the read syscall we need to call file_read so we acquire the file lock, but
if our buffer is not writable and we try to write to the buffer from our read
syscall we will have a page fault. The page fault will then try to terminate the
process, but when terminating the process the file lock will once again try to
be acquired. But since the process already holds the file lock we would reach
another deadlock. To prevent this hold and wait case we made sure to check that
the buffer for read is writable before we even call the syscall.


>> B6: A page fault in process P can cause another process Q's frame >> to be
evicted.  How do you ensure that Q cannot access or modify >> the page during
the eviction process?  How do you avoid a race >> between P evicting Q's frame
and Q faulting the page back in?

To ensure that Q does not access the page during process P, we destroy the
mapping that Q holds to the physical frame. We also set the evicting frame's
in_memory value to true and pin the frame. Then when Q tries to access the same
address, it will trigger a fault. To prevent Q from accessing the frame in this
fault, we must block Q untill P's fault has finished. In our
vm_page_fault_helper function which actually faults the page back in it first
checks if the frame's in_memory is true and if it is. The vm_page_fault_helper
does nothing. And since we set in_memory to true for our evicting page Q will
not be able to fault the page back in Then it is safe to unblock Q. Also because
we pin the evicting frame no other frame can evict it including Q. During
eviction we also hold the frame table lock so that no other process including Q
can change the frame table.


>> B7: Suppose a page fault in process P causes a page to be read from >> the
file system or swap.  How do you ensure that a second process Q >> cannot
interfere by e.g. attempting to evict the frame while it is >> still being read
in?

We ensure that a second process Q cannot interfere by pinning the frame that is
being read into from the file system or swap. In our
page-fault/vm_page_fault_helper right after we call our frame_allocate function
and set the frame we pin the frame. Then only after page_fault and
vm_page_fault_helper finishes what it needs to i.e reading from file system or
swap we unpin the frame. Our get_victim_frame function never chooses a pinned
frame as a frame to evict which means that a second procces Q cannot interfere
with our process P which is reading from a file system or swap.

>> B8: Explain how you handle access to paged-out pages that occur >> during
system calls.  Do you use page faults to bring in pages (as >> in user
programs), or do you have a mechanism for "locking" frames >> into physical
memory, or do you use some other design?  How do you >> gracefully handle
attempted accesses to invalid virtual addresses?

Our paged-out pages that occur during system calls are handled with page_fault
and our vm_page_fault_helper with demand-paging, along with a pinning mechanism.
If we try to access a paged-out page in our system call, we first check for a
valid virtual address. We already checked invalid virtual addresses in project 3
with page_fault with checking if (!user && fault_addr < PHYS_BASE). However this
is not enough for syscall virtual address checks when virtual address does not
have a physical mapping (not_present == true). In project 3 however we may have
a virtual address that does not have a physical mapping (not_present == true).
To check the validity of this virtual address we use our helper fucntion
find_spte (fault_addr); to see if the address is a valid virtual address by
seeing if we can find it in the supplemental page table. If its in the table
then it is a valid address that we can get from demand paging (even if we have
to swap back out). And if it doesnt exist in the spt, and isn't valid to grow
the stack then the address would be invalid and we would terminate the process
as the address is invalid.

Once the address is confirmed to be valid we use our vm_page_fault_helper
function to do demand paging based on the type of page of virtual address was
reffering to and allocate a physical frame for the virtual address. If the page
is of type VM_BIN or VM_FILE we load the respective file and if it is VM_ANON we
swap back in the page.

We also have a pinning mechanism to "lock" frames into memory. Our pinning
mechanism is designed so that any pinned frame cannot be evicted. As explained
in B7 we use our pinning system to ensure another processes cannot attempt to
evict a frame while it is being read in. Additionally, we also pin frames of
user resources that the kernel needs to use so that a page fault wont occur and
cause a deadlock. We mainly implement this for the read and write system calls.
We cant let pages with user resouces be evicted while a system call is using
them or else we will run into a deadlock. For both read and write syscalls we
need to pin all pages inside the buffer. For example if we call read syscall we
will need to acquire the filesys lock and call file_read. But if the buffer is
evicted our vm system will page fault to bring back the buffer pages. But the
page fault will try to acquire the filesys lock again and cause a deadlock. That
is why we pin user resources used such as buffer for read and write syscalls and
unpin them once the syscall is done. (Note: same explanation as B5)

As explained above we gracefully handle attempted access to invalid virtual
addresses by seeing if it exists in the spt, if it doesnt we try to grow the
stack. But if all fail we will terminate the process.



---- RATIONALE ----

>> B9: A single lock for the whole VM system would make >> synchronization easy,
but limit parallelism.  On the other hand, >> using many locks complicates
synchronization and raises the >> possibility for deadlock but allows for high
parallelism.  Explain >> where your design falls along this continuum and why
you chose to >> design it this way.

Our implementation uses three locks: frame_table_lock (for the physical frame
list), swap_lock (for the swap bitmap) and filesys_lock (for file operations).
This places us in the middle of the continuum as we avoid using only a single
global lock while also not using many locks. 

Using a single global VM lock would limit the system to one memory operation at
a time. Although it could avoid all deadlocks, it would cripple the runtime and
system performance if multiple threads are trying to access files.

On the other hand, using many locks could maximize parallelism but would be
enormously complex. Acquring multiple locks in the correct order, avoiding
deadlocks when evicting a chain of dependent pages would require a very
intricate algorithm that would be unreasonably complicated to implement with
very little benefits in the scope of this course. 

Our middle-continuum design avoids deadlocks while keeping the locking order
simple and understandable. The frame_table_lock and swap_lock are only acquired
during page allocation/eviction and swap in/out. The filesys_lock is only
acquired for disk I/O operations only. 

We chose this design because it achieves the primary goal of no deadlocks and no
race conditions without sacrificing the parallelism that is required for the
tests. 

