>> C2: Describe how memory mapped files integrate into your virtual >> memory
subsystem.  Explain how the page fault and eviction >> processes differ between
swap pages and other pages.

each mmap file is represented by an struct mmap_file object. this object
contains a list of every supp_page_table_entry that file mapping spans.
furthermore each process tracks its mappings in its thread object, a list field
mmap_list.

when mmap() is called it validates the request, then computes how many virtual
pages (spte) are needed. Then it sets those tables objects up as VM_FILE and
calculates read_bytes, zero_bytes, and sets up the rest of the fields
accordingly.

then when it page faults, it hits the VM_FILE path and gets loaded in via the
page fault handler. 

for unmappping, we iterate through the mmap_file object that corresponds to the
mapping. during the iteration we write the page back to file / disk location its
recorded at, then clear the page table etnry and free the frame. The spte's are
all removed from the processes page table and freed. This also happens during
process_exit.

the page fault and eviction process differ between swap pages and other pages.
For swap pages, (type VM_ANON), it has no home in the disk, when it is evicted,
its data must be written to somewhere or else it is lost. We create an list of
swap slots. Each slot represents where the data is written upon eviction to and
each spte carries a swap slot field. 

for the other pages, they all have backing / copies of themselves so the
eviction process is different. FOr VM_BIN, since it always has a copy, eviction
just discards it and doesn't save anything. However, if written, to it must not
override the original file, so it is saved to a swap slot, similiar to the
VM_ANON case.

For VM_FILE, it is meant to be written too, so munmap and eviction will write
back the changed files contents directly to its physical location. 


>> C3: Explain how you determine whether a new file mapping overlaps >> any
existing segment.

Overlap checking is done directly in the mmap() function. we loop over all the
spte's that the file would need and make sure they are all free before
allocating annything. If not, we return -1. 

---- RATIONALE ----

>> C4: Mappings created with "mmap" have similar semantics to those of >> data
demand-paged from executables, except that "mmap" mappings are >> written back
to their original files, not to swap.  This implies >> that much of their
implementation can be shared.  Explain why your >> implementation either does or
does not share much of the code for >> the two situations.

Our implementation shares the loading follow between VM_BIN and VM_FILE exactly
in the page fault helper. This is because the two are identical in the process
of bringing the page into the physical memorr, both are file backed, have a
source file, a byte offset. All we need to do Is track the correct read_bytes
and zero_bytes. 

The two differ in the write back or eviction site. When the VM_BIN is evicted it
needs to be written to a swap slot if edited else, discarded. VM_FILE however
will get written back to its originall mapped file if it has been modified.



